From e6d916eadb95f9b36a53e22ead5f4673bf3009a2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emir Turkes <eturkes@bu.edu>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 16:56:39 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] fix(proxy): hard-terminate timed-out image workers and honor
 passthrough for images
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Timed-out image-compression jobs left their RapidOCR/ONNX worker
processes running forever: _reset_image_pool() used
shutdown(wait=False, cancel_futures=True), which cancels only queued
futures. Every timeout then built a fresh one-worker pool, so ~1 GiB /
65-thread workers accumulated until the host ran out of CPU and RAM
(observed live: 10 concurrent workers, 10.5 GiB aggregate RSS, worker
lifetimes >20 min past their 30 s timeout).

Pool lifecycle:
- Capture the exact pool a job ran on and retire that pool on failure.
  Retirement is compare-and-swap (a stale timeout can never tear down a
  newer replacement pool) and idempotent.
- Hard-stop retired workers: terminate_workers() then kill_workers()
  after a bounded grace on Python 3.14+, with a guarded private-table
  fallback for 3.10–3.13. Signals are sent BEFORE shutdown() —
  shutdown() drops the process table and turns them into no-ops.
- Admission control: one in-flight job for the one-worker pool; extra
  requests fail open after a short wait instead of queueing, so a
  queued job's execution timeout can no longer expire before it runs.

Operator controls:
- New --no-image-optimize / HEADROOM_NO_IMAGE_OPTIMIZE (click + argparse
  paths) disables the image path outright.
- --no-optimize (global passthrough) now implies image_optimize=False,
  enforced both in config wiring and in ImageCompressionDecision via a
  new optimize_disabled gate.
- --compressor/HEADROOM_COMPRESSORS selections without "image" now also
  close the proxy-level image gate instead of only the in-router flag.

OCR bounds:
- RapidOCR sessions are created with intra/inter-op threads pinned to 1
  (HEADROOM_OCR_INTRA_THREADS / HEADROOM_OCR_INTER_THREADS to override,
  v1 and v3 APIs both handled, fail-open to an unbounded engine).
